          "chain_quality_note": "Multi-link chain through seven transmitters from Ibn Abbas to Saduq",
          "has_chain": true,
          "matn_start_word": 57,
          "matn_summary": "The Prophet (SAWW) describes an angel named Sakha'il who brings five divine writs (barawat) from the Lord of the Worlds for those who perform the five daily obligatory prayers. Each writ contains God's unique declaration: for Fajr, He is 'the Everlasting' and forgives sins until Dhuhr; for Dhuhr, He is 'the All-Powerful' and converts evil deeds into good; for Asr, He is 'the Majestic' and forbids their bodies from the Fire; for Maghrib, He is 'the Compeller, the Grand, the Most High' and promises to fulfil their desires on Resurrection Day; for Isha, He is 'the One besides whom there is no deity' and declares His pleasure with them. After Isha, Sakha'il calls three times and every angel in the seven heavens seeks forgiveness for the worshippers. Whoever performs the night prayer sincerely is surrounded by nine rows of angels stretching from East to West, and earns ranks equal to their uncountable number. The narrator Mansur reports that al-Rabi' ibn Badr would exclaim after this hadith: 'Where are you, O heedless one, from this generosity?'",
